1. What you may do

   Free of charge, and for as long as you keep to this licence, you may:

   a. install and use the Software on computers that you own or control, or
      that you have been given to use (for example by your employer), for
      any lawful purpose, including at work, within what the Terms of Use
      say about how the app should and should not be used;
   b. read the source code, and change it for your own use on those
      computers;
   c. make the copies that installing, using and backing up the Software
      need;
   d. tell other people about the project and point them to its page, so
      that they can get the Software there themselves;
   e. let a backup service, or someone who looks after your computer, handle
      your copy on your behalf, and include short excerpts, screenshots or
      log lines when you report a problem to the project, as long as nobody
      else gets a usable copy of the Software.

   This licence is personal to you, non-exclusive and non-transferable. It
   covers each copy that the maintainer gives you, or that you download from
   the project's page while the maintainer offers it there, including later
   versions that you get in the same way. The
   maintainer may stop offering the Software at any time; copies that you
   already hold stay licensed until this licence ends under section 8.

2. What you may not do

   Unless the maintainer has given you permission in writing, you may not:

   a. give, send, publish, upload, mirror or otherwise make available the
      Software or any part of it, changed or unchanged, to anyone else. This
      does not stop you sending something to the project through its page
      (see section 3), or doing what section 1e allows. Where the project's
      page is public, GitHub's own terms let its users view the page and
      make a copy of it on GitHub (a "fork"); this licence does not take
      that away, and it applies to every such copy;
   b. sell, rent, lease or sublicense the Software, or charge for it or for
      access to it;
   c. use the Software's source code, or a substantial part of it, in another
      program or work that you give or make available to anyone else;
   d. remove or change, in any copy, a copyright, licence or terms notice, or
      the step in which the Terms of Use are accepted, other than in a copy
      that only you use;
   e. use the name "Hold to talk" or its icon in a way that suggests that
      something else is this project, or is approved by it.

